+# Receipts stopped
+
+For the volunteer on call when members report that no receipt emails arrive. Starts the stopped worker so the queued
+receipts go out; until then, members who paid get no receipt. Written after the second time it happened, in
+September: the first time, the only volunteer who knew the fix was asleep and members waited until noon. Owner: the
+volunteer on call; Tomás if the worker will not stay up.
+
+## Before you begin
+
+- A shell on the larder server, with `sudo` for `systemctl`, and `psql` access to the `larder` database.
+- A copy of this repository there, for `scripts/restart-receipt-worker.sh`.
+
+Receipts are sent by [the worker, the systemd unit `larder-worker`](../architecture.md#parts). The deploy restarts
+only the web app, so a deploy that stops the worker leaves it stopped: in September the worker died at 23:02, right
+after that evening's deploy.
+
+## Steps
+
+1. Run `scripts/restart-receipt-worker.sh`. It shows the worker's status and the jobs by status, starts the worker,
+   then prints the jobs left to do every minute until none is left.
+
+   Expected: first `inactive (dead)` and a count of `todo` jobs, in September 212; then the `todo` count falling, by
+   about 40 a minute; then `Queue drained.`
+2. Read the final job counts.
+
+   Expected: `todo` 0 and `doing` 0. A few `failed` jobs are expected: receipts that bounced because the member has
+   no valid email address. They need nothing from you.
+3. Ask a member who reported the problem whether a receipt has arrived.
+
+   Expected: it has.
+
+## If it fails
+
+- The script says the worker is already running: the worker is not the cause. Run
+  `journalctl -u larder-worker -n 50` and send the output to Tomás.
+- The script says the worker stopped again: it prints the worker's last 50 log lines; send them to Tomás in
+  #larder-ops.
+- Many `failed` jobs, not a few: send the counts and `journalctl -u larder-worker -n 50` to Tomás.
